dict(slug="how-to-get-first-users-for-an-indie-app", date="2026-10-22",
 title="How to get first users for an indie app (without an audience)",
 desc="How to get first users for an indie app when nobody knows you yet: find where your users already talk, reach out one by one, and turn early users into a loop.",
 body="""
<p>If you are wondering how to get first users for an indie app, you are probably past the fun part. The app works, the landing page is up, and the analytics show a flat line. This guide is for that moment: no audience, no ad budget, and a product that deserves a fair shot.</p>
<p>The short version: your first users will not come from a launch. They come from you going to the places your users already are, talking to people one at a time, and making it easy for the ones who like it to bring someone else.</p>
<h2>1. Write down exactly who the first user is</h2>
<p>"Freelancers" or "students" is not a user. "A freelance designer who sends three to five invoices a month and hates spreadsheets" is. The narrower the description, the easier every next step becomes, because you know which forums, subreddits, Discord servers and newsletters that person reads.</p>
<p>A useful test: could you name five real places online where this person asks questions about the problem your app solves? If not, narrow the description until you can.</p>
<h2>2. Go where the problem is already being discussed</h2>
<p>People rarely search for a new app. They search for a fix to a problem. That means the best early channels are the threads where someone is already describing the pain:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Niche communities.</strong> Subreddits, Discord and Slack groups, Facebook groups and forums for the profession or hobby, not for founders.</li>
<li><strong>Q&amp;A threads.</strong> Search Reddit, Stack Exchange sites and Quora for the exact phrases your users would type ("how do I track", "is there a tool that"). Old threads still get read through search.</li>
<li><strong>Comment sections.</strong> Blog posts and videos that review competing tools attract exactly the people who are shopping.</li>
</ul>
<p>Read each community's rules first. Many ban self-promotion outright. In those places, help without linking, and put the app in your profile. In places that allow it, answer the question fully first and mention your app at the end, saying plainly that you built it.</p>
<h2>3. Do things that don't scale, on purpose</h2>
<p>Your first users are worth far more than their number suggests, because they tell you what is broken and what to build next. Treat them that way:</p>
<ul>
<li>Send short personal messages to people who described the problem in public. Mention their post, offer the app free, and ask for ten minutes of feedback in return.</li>
<li>Onboard people yourself. A quick call or a screen recording made for one person often reveals more than a week of analytics.</li>
<li>Reply to every signup with a personal email. Ask one question: "What were you hoping this would do?"</li>
</ul>
<p>None of this scales, and that is fine. At this stage you are not optimizing a funnel; you are finding out whether anyone cares enough to come back.</p>
<h2>4. Make the landing page answer three questions fast</h2>
<p>When someone does click through, they decide in seconds. The page should answer, above the fold:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>What is it?</strong> One plain sentence, in the user's words, not yours.</li>
<li><strong>Is it for me?</strong> Name the person or situation directly.</li>
<li><strong>What does it cost and what do I do next?</strong> A visible price (or "free") and one clear button.</li>
</ol>
<p>A short screen recording or a few honest screenshots usually beat an illustrated hero image. Show the real app doing the real job.</p>
<h2>5. Get listed where people browse for tools</h2>
<p>Directories and marketplaces will not make you famous, but they add steady, low-effort discovery and a few backlinks that help search engines find you. Prioritize ones that fit your category: app stores for mobile, itch.io for games, Gumroad's discover pages for digital products, and indie maker directories for everything else. Write each listing for that audience instead of pasting the same blurb everywhere.</p>
<h2>6. Build in public, but give people something to react to</h2>
<p>Posting progress on X, Bluesky, Mastodon or LinkedIn works best when each post is useful or interesting on its own: a before-and-after, a small lesson, a decision you are stuck on. "Shipped v1.2" gets scrolled past. "I removed signup and activation went up, here's why I think that happened" starts a conversation. Ask questions. People like to weigh in on choices.</p>
<h2>7. Turn first users into the next users</h2>
<p>Once a handful of people use the app regularly, make sharing natural:</p>
<ul>
<li>Ask happy users directly whether they know one other person with the same problem.</li>
<li>Add sharing where it fits the product: a public link, an export with a small "made with" note, an invite for teammates.</li>
<li>Ask for a review on the platform where it matters most (an app store, a directory, a community thread).</li>
</ul>
<h2>8. Track the one number that matters</h2>
<p>Early on, total signups can mislead you. Watch whether people come back: how many of last week's new users did something meaningful again this week. If that number is near zero, more traffic will not fix it. Talk to the people who left and fix the product first. If people are coming back, that is your signal to push harder on the channels that brought them.</p>
<h2>A simple weekly routine</h2>
<ul>
<li>Monday: find ten fresh threads where your problem is being discussed and answer the ones you can genuinely help with.</li>
<li>Tuesday to Thursday: message or email five people individually, and ship one improvement based on what you heard.</li>
<li>Friday: post one honest build-in-public update and submit to one new relevant directory.</li>
</ul>
<p>Keep it up for a month before judging the results. First users are earned slowly, and the habits that get you the first twenty are the same ones that get you the first two hundred.</p>
<h2>Get your app in front of people</h2>
<p>One easy step you can take today: <a href="https://digitalundergroundstore.com/community/submit/">list your project free on the Digital Underground Developer Marketplace</a>. There is no fee and no cut of sales, and visitors go straight to your own site. If you like a bit of friendly competition, you can also <a href="https://digitalundergroundstore.com/league/">enter the Maker League</a> to get your project seen by other makers.</p>
"""),
dict(slug="how-to-find-beta-testers-for-your-app", date="2026-10-22",
 title="How to find beta testers for your app (and get useful feedback)",
 desc="How to find beta testers for your app as a solo maker: where to look, how to ask, how to run a small beta, and how to turn feedback into fixes.",
 body="""
<p>Knowing how to find beta testers for your app matters more than most makers expect. A beta with the wrong people, or with no structure, produces polite silence. A small beta with the right people tells you what to fix before strangers see it. Here is how to find those people and get feedback you can actually use.</p>
<h2>Decide what you need to learn first</h2>
<p>Before recruiting anyone, write down the two or three questions the beta should answer. For example:</p>
<ul>
<li>Can a new user complete the main task without help?</li>
<li>Where do people get stuck or give up?</li>
<li>Would they keep using it next week, and would they pay for it?</li>
</ul>
<p>These questions decide who you recruit and what you ask. A beta testing "everything" usually tests nothing.</p>
<h2>How many testers you actually need</h2>
<p>For finding usability problems, a handful of people watched closely is often enough to spot the biggest issues. For testing whether people stick around, you need more, enough that a few dropouts do not decide the result. A practical approach is to start with a small group you talk to personally, fix what they find, and then open a wider round.</p>
<h2>Where to find beta testers</h2>
<h3>People who already have the problem</h3>
<p>The best testers are not other developers (unless you build developer tools). They are people who feel the pain your app solves. Look in the communities they use: hobby or profession subreddits, Discord servers, forums, Facebook groups and newsletters. Read the rules, then post a short, honest request: what the app does, who it is for, what you are asking testers to do and how long it takes.</p>
<h3>Your own network, carefully</h3>
<p>Friends and family are good for catching crashes and confusing copy, but they are too kind to tell you the product is not useful. Use them for the first smoke test, then move on to strangers.</p>
<h3>Maker and launch communities</h3>
<p>Indie maker forums, build-in-public circles on X or Bluesky, and maker directories that let you mark a project as being in beta are full of people willing to try new things. Their feedback is sharp on onboarding and polish, though they may not be your long-term customers.</p>
<h3>Platform tools</h3>
<p>If you ship on mobile, use the built-in channels: TestFlight on iOS and internal or closed testing tracks on Google Play. They handle distribution, so testers install with a link instead of fiddling with files. For desktop and web apps, a private link or a simple invite code does the same job.</p>
<h3>Your waitlist and existing users</h3>
<p>If you collected emails on a landing page, those people already raised their hand. Email them personally, not as a blast, and invite a small batch at a time.</p>
<h2>How to ask so people say yes</h2>
<p>A good beta invite is short and specific:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>What it is</strong> in one sentence.</li>
<li><strong>Who it is for</strong>, so the wrong people self-select out.</li>
<li><strong>The ask</strong>: "Use it for one real task this week and answer three questions" is far easier to agree to than "give me feedback".</li>
<li><strong>What they get</strong>: free access during the beta, a discount or lifetime plan later, credit in the changelog, or simply early access. Keep any promise you make.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Run the beta with a little structure</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Give a first task.</strong> Tell testers exactly what to try first. Open-ended exploration produces vague notes.</li>
<li><strong>Make feedback effortless.</strong> One channel only: a short form, a feedback button in the app, or a small chat group. Ask for screenshots when something breaks.</li>
<li><strong>Watch a few sessions live.</strong> Ask two or three testers to share their screen and think out loud while you stay quiet. You will see confusion they would never write down.</li>
<li><strong>Follow up after a few days.</strong> Ask whether they came back on their own, and if not, why.</li>
<li><strong>Close the loop.</strong> When you fix something a tester reported, tell them. It is the single best way to keep testers engaged for the next round.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Questions that produce useful answers</h2>
<ul>
<li>"What did you expect to happen when you first opened it?"</li>
<li>"Where did you hesitate or get stuck?"</li>
<li>"What would you use instead if this didn't exist?"</li>
<li>"If it were gone tomorrow, how would you feel?"</li>
<li>"What would make you recommend it to someone?"</li>
</ul>
<p>Avoid leading questions like "Did you like the new dashboard?" People will say yes to be nice. Ask about behavior and expectations instead of opinions.</p>
<h2>Turning feedback into decisions</h2>
<p>Put every piece of feedback in one list and tag it: bug, confusion, missing feature, or not for me. Fix bugs and confusion first; they hurt everyone. Look for patterns in missing features before building anything, since one loud request is not a roadmap. And take "not for me" seriously: if many testers say it, you may be recruiting the wrong people, or the positioning needs work.</p>
<h2>Common beta mistakes</h2>
<ul>
<li>Waiting until the app is polished. Testers forgive rough edges if the core idea works.</li>
<li>Inviting everyone at once. Small batches let you fix problems between rounds.</li>
<li>Letting the beta drift for months. Set an end date and a clear next step.</li>
<li>Not saying thank you. A short personal note goes a long way.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Find testers on the marketplace</h2>
<p>You can <a href="https://digitalundergroundstore.com/community/submit/">list your project free on the Digital Underground Developer Marketplace</a> with the stage set to "Beta / testing" and a status label like "Needs testers", so curious visitors know you are looking for feedback. To get in front of other makers too, <a href="https://digitalundergroundstore.com/league/">enter the Maker League</a>.</p>
"""),
dict(slug="how-to-price-your-side-project", date="2026-10-22",
 title="How to price your side project: a practical guide for indie makers",
 desc="How to price your side project without guessing: pick a pricing model, anchor on the value you deliver, test with real buyers, and raise prices with confidence.",
 body="""
<p>Learning how to price your side project is one of the most uncomfortable parts of shipping. Price too low and you attract people who will never value the work. Price too high without explaining why and nobody tries it. The good news is that pricing is not a one-time guess. It is a decision you can test, adjust and improve, and this guide walks through how.</p>
<h2>Start with the value, not your costs</h2>
<p>Your hosting bill and the hours you spent building are real, but buyers do not see them. They see what the product does for them. Ask:</p>
<ul>
<li>How much time or money does this save the user each month?</li>
<li>What are they using today instead, and what does that cost (in money, time or frustration)?</li>
<li>Is this for work, where someone else may pay, or for personal use, where people are more price-sensitive?</li>
</ul>
<p>A tool that saves a freelancer an hour every week can support a much higher price than a nice-to-have utility, even if the second one was harder to build. Costs set your floor; value sets your ceiling.</p>
<h2>Pick a pricing model that matches how people use it</h2>
<h3>One-time purchase</h3>
<p>Works well for tools people use occasionally, templates, ebooks, games and offline apps. It is easy to understand and easy to say yes to. The trade-off is that income stops when new sales stop, so plan for paid major upgrades or new products.</p>
<h3>Subscription</h3>
<p>Fits products with ongoing costs or ongoing value: anything that syncs, hosts data, or gets regular updates people rely on. Offering both monthly and a discounted yearly plan is common and gives buyers a choice.</p>
<h3>Free plan plus paid upgrade</h3>
<p>Useful when the product gets better the more people use it, or when trying it is the best way to sell it. Be strict about what is free: the free plan should be genuinely useful but leave a clear reason to upgrade, such as limits on volume, advanced features or commercial use.</p>
<h3>Pay what you want</h3>
<p>Good for building goodwill and an audience, especially for creative work. Set a suggested price; many buyers will use it as a guide.</p>
<h2>Look at the alternatives, then position yourself</h2>
<p>Make a short list of the products your users would consider instead, including the "do it manually" option. Note their prices and what you do better or differently. You do not need to be the cheapest. Being the simplest, the most focused, the offline option or the one with no account required are all reasons people pay more. Pricing far below every alternative can actually make buyers suspicious of quality.</p>
<h2>Keep it simple</h2>
<p>For a side project, one or two options is usually enough. Every extra tier is a decision the buyer has to make, and confusion stops sales. If you add tiers later, make the differences obvious and name them by who they are for ("Personal", "Pro", "Team") rather than by abstract labels.</p>
<h2>Test with real buyers, not surveys</h2>
<p>Asking "would you pay $10 for this?" produces unreliable answers, because saying yes costs nothing. Better signals:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Put a price on it early.</strong> A real checkout, even for a beta or presale, tells you more than any poll.</li>
<li><strong>Talk to people who bought and people who didn't.</strong> Ask buyers what nearly stopped them, and ask visitors who left what they expected the price to be.</li>
<li><strong>Change one thing at a time.</strong> If you adjust the price, keep the page and the traffic sources similar for a while so you can tell what made the difference.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Raising prices without drama</h2>
<p>Most makers underprice their first version and should raise prices as the product improves. A few ways to do it well:</p>
<ul>
<li>Announce the change ahead of time and let people buy at the current price until a set date.</li>
<li>Keep existing customers on their original price if you can. It builds loyalty and costs little.</li>
<li>Tie the increase to something concrete: new features, better support, more templates.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Make the price page do its job</h2>
<ul>
<li>Show the price clearly. Hiding it behind a signup makes people assume it is expensive.</li>
<li>Say exactly what is included and whether it is one-time or recurring.</li>
<li>Explain the refund policy in one line. A fair, clear refund policy reduces the risk of buying.</li>
<li>Answer the two or three questions buyers always ask, right next to the button.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Discounts: use them sparingly</h2>
<p>Launch discounts, holiday sales and student pricing can all work, but constant discounts teach buyers to wait. Give every discount a clear reason and an end date, and honor that end date.</p>
<h2>A quick pricing checklist</h2>
<ol>
<li>Write down the main value the product delivers and who it is for.</li>
<li>List the alternatives and their prices.</li>
<li>Choose one model and at most two options.</li>
<li>Launch with a real price, even if it feels a little high.</li>
<li>Talk to buyers and non-buyers, then revisit the price after you have real data.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Show your price where buyers are browsing</h2>
<p>When you are ready, <a href="https://digitalundergroundstore.com/community/submit/">list your project free on the Digital Underground Developer Marketplace</a>. Your price label appears on the card, buyers check out on your own site, and there is no fee or cut of sales. You can also <a href="https://digitalundergroundstore.com/league/">enter the Maker League</a> to put your project in front of other makers.</p>
"""),
